# EVERY NAMEPLATE CALLING THE DUTY-CYCLE QUESTION MALFORMED, BESIDE WHETHER ITS UNIT RUNS ON A
# PERIOD. One row per examined nameplate, `violates` where the unit does have one.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE RULE: a window is notApplicable only where the unit has no period. `12 people`
#    has no period, so "five days" has nothing to be five days of and the question really is
#    malformed. `4 GPU-hours per week` has one, so the question is perfectly answerable -- and
#    `notApplicable` there is a refusal dressed as a category error.
#
# ⛔⛔ A NAMEPLATE WITH NO UNIT IS NOT EXAMINED, AND THAT IS THE REPAIR. `refutation`'s
#    `object-storage` files its amount `notApplicable` -- there is no unit at all -- and its
#    window `notApplicable` too. A NULL unit matches no `LIKE`, so a semi-join against a parsed
#    denominator passes it, on the grounds that a unit which does not exist has no denominator.
#    That is an unknown answered as a no. layers/absorber.sqlc states the principle: a question
#    nobody can answer gets NO ROW rather than a pass.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE SEMI-JOIN READS A FILED FACT AND NEVER A PARSED ONE. Against free text,
#    `LIKE '% per %' OR LIKE '% por %'` guesses the denominator out of the unit string and
#    silently decides that `semana` IS `week`: a reader's judgement written in SQL, which no
#    comment on the query side can fix.
#
#    It is also the wrong predicate: a `LIKE` finds a DENOMINATOR, and this rule needs a
#    PERIOD. `pm:StatedDenominator` separates them, so the semi-join is against
#    units/with_a_period.sqlc and `GPU-hour per GPU` does not count as a rate.
-- pm:Divisibility/window with pm:Absent reason="notApplicable", against pm:Claim/pm:denominator.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT w.filing, w.layer,
           r.filing IS NOT NULL AS violates,
           CASE WHEN r.filing IS NOT NULL
                THEN format('%s runs on a period, so the duty cycle question is answerable',
                            w.amount_unit)
                ELSE format('%s has no period under the line, so the question really is malformed',
                            w.amount_unit)
           END AS detail
    FROM      (
        :compose(layers/windows.sqlc)
    ) w
    LEFT JOIN (
        :compose(units/with_a_period.sqlc)
    ) r USING (filing, layer)
    WHERE w.window_absent = 'notApplicable'
      AND w.amount_unit IS NOT NULL
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'window_not_applicable_on_a_rate'
